Sorry for the intrusion, but I'd like to share an idea. I don't have an in-depth knowledge of the implementation, but I think it's similar to this:"
```php
// implementation with recursion problem:
public function getErrors(): array
{
    $errors = $this->errors;
    foreach ($this->linkedEntities as $innerEntity) {
        array_push($errors, ...$innerEntity->getErrors());
    }
    return $errors;
}
```
You can refactor it as follows, maintaining the previous compatibility without using static properties or an internal state within the class:

```php
public function getErrors(): array
{
    $storage = new \SplObjectStorage(); // start new "context" to collect errors
    $this->internalGetErrors($storage);

    $errors = []; // process collected errors
    $storage->rewind();
    while ($storage->valid()) {
        array_push($errors, ...$storage->getInfo());
        $storage->next();
    }
    return $errors;
}

// collecting errors: make private for internal use only
private function internalGetErrors(\SplObjectStorage $storage): void
{
    if ($storage->contains($this)) { //checks if this has been visited
        return;
    }
    $storage->attach($this, $this->errors);
    foreach ($this->linkedEntities as $innerEntity) {
        $innerEntity->internalGetErrors($storage);
    }
}
```

This is only an idea and, obviously, does not reflect the current implementation.

> While this would work, it would have the drawback of modifying the behavior quite significantly. As I wrote in my previous comments:
> 
The behavior has not been retained? The method would still fulfills the responsibility of returning its own errors and those of related entities, but now avoids circular dependencies. Whoever overrides the method should understand that they need to deal with the same challenges, but on their own.

Maybe I'm talking nonsense because I don't understand the implementation details, but my idea is just a suggestion focused on avoiding static properties and extra attributes that would need to be cleaned up. Here's a simplified version that might help you (but, the previous one seems more interesting):
```php
public function getErrors(?SplObjectStorage $visited = null): array
{
    $visited ??= new SplObjectStorage();
    if ($visited->contains($this)) {
        return [];
    }
    $visited->attach($this);
    $errors = $this->errors;
    foreach ($this->linkedEntities as $innerEntity) {
        array_push($errors, ...$innerEntity->getErrors($visited));
    }
    return $errors;
}
```
